WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.535 --> 00:00:04.010
(orchestral music)

00:00:04.010 --> 00:00:06.770
- [Narrator] Tea is the most
popular drink in the world.

00:00:06.770 --> 00:00:09.370
And there are countless
ways we make and consume it.

00:00:11.230 --> 00:00:13.210
But if you want to drink
tea in the oldest way

00:00:13.210 --> 00:00:15.800
we still know of, you'll
first need to hike deep

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:18.590
into the remote Tea
Mountains of southwest China

00:00:18.590 --> 00:00:21.150
and hand-pick leaves
and buds from tea trees

00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:23.490
that are hundreds of years old.

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:26.330
It's a long way to go
for authentic Chinese tea,

00:00:26.330 --> 00:00:28.343
but one person is doing it.

00:00:34.690 --> 00:00:35.720
- My name's Shunan Teng.

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:37.110
I'm the owner of Tea Drunk,

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:39.253
which is a teahouse in New York City.

00:00:40.150 --> 00:00:43.020
We specialize in historic Chinese tea.

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:46.090
Historic teas really represent
the pinnacle of tea culture.

00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:48.610
And these teas are
usually not mass produced

00:00:48.610 --> 00:00:52.750
and they try to be as authentic
to the tea as possible.

00:00:52.750 --> 00:00:56.730
It’s almost like a masterpiece of
music played versus a practice

00:00:56.730 --> 00:01:00.153
or a original piece of
artwork versus the copies.

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:03.020
- [Narrator] The masterpiece
tea, so to speak,

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:06.010
comes from ancient tea
trees that grow in the wild.

00:01:06.010 --> 00:01:08.570
They only bud for 15 days out of the year.

00:01:08.570 --> 00:01:11.800
So Shunan must race each
spring to find the trees

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:14.860
and harvest them in time with
the help of local farmers.

00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:17.660
- The best tea trees are always on some

00:01:17.660 --> 00:01:19.490
of the hardest to get to places.

00:01:19.490 --> 00:01:22.910
Because they need to be
on very steep slopes.

00:01:22.910 --> 00:01:25.610
We usually motorcycle a little bit.

00:01:25.610 --> 00:01:27.780
Sometimes we tread water.

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:29.363
Sometimes we climb.

00:01:30.790 --> 00:01:33.020
In this region, it's not
uncommon for tea trees

00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:35.410
to grow for several hundred years.

00:01:35.410 --> 00:01:38.430
These tea trees right
now, is at it's prime

00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:41.654
and this is what tea
is meant to taste like.

00:01:41.654 --> 00:01:43.033
Mm, tasty.

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:48.220
Once we pick the tea,
we need to spread it out

00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:51.830
in a whole area where
the water can travel out

00:01:51.830 --> 00:01:53.483
before we can wok fry the tea.

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:56.800
This step is to kill the enzymes,

00:01:56.800 --> 00:01:59.950
so the tea fermentation can be stopped.

00:01:59.950 --> 00:02:04.083
Then we take the tea leaves
out and then we roll the tea.

00:02:04.930 --> 00:02:06.650
Once the sun dry the tea,

00:02:06.650 --> 00:02:09.900
you need to pick out any discoloration.

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:12.520
This sorting process usually
takes months to finish

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:14.460
because we do have to do them one by one

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:16.690
with every single tea.

00:02:16.690 --> 00:02:18.350
- [Narrator] Months will
go by before this tea

00:02:18.350 --> 00:02:20.040
is ready to be poured out.

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:23.280
But the journey is well
worth it to Shunan.

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:26.580
- Those extreme fine points in taste

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:28.100
that tea offers us,

00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:30.450
I think it provides us [with] a level of joy

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:32.410
that's beyond anything else.

00:02:32.410 --> 00:02:35.830
And my job here is to preserve this art.

00:02:35.830 --> 00:02:38.323
And hopefully even push
it to a new height.

